decisions:
  - id: D-fork-heavy-skills
    decision: Fork 8 skills (preflight, postflight, sentinel, doctor, observe, ripple, review, handoff) to run in isolated subagents
    rationale: Heavy skills consume 3-7K tokens in the main conversation. Forking keeps history clean and lets the skill do its multi-step work without cluttering context. Skills that need conversation history (lore, protocol) stay inline.
  - id: D-agent-routing-map
    decision: Route analysis skills to paradigm:architect, compliance skills to paradigm:reviewer, data-fetching to Explore
    rationale: 'Paradigm already defines these agent types. Natural mapping: architects analyze impact, reviewers check compliance, Explore fetches data. No new agent types needed.'
  - id: D-manual-only-setup
    decision: Set disable-model-invocation on init, shift, and scan
    rationale: These are explicit user actions that create files or rebuild state. Auto-triggering could be destructive or confusing. User should consciously choose to run them.
  - id: D-graceful-degradation
    decision: Ship v2 frontmatter knowing older Claude Code versions ignore unknown fields
    rationale: No breaking change. Skills work as v1 on older versions, gain v2 features on newer versions. Zero migration burden for users.
errors_encountered:
  - description: Plugin version detection returned 3.9.0 instead of 3.23.0 due to alphabetical sort of cache directory names
    resolution: Replaced .sort() with numeric semver comparator splitting on dots and comparing as integers
    time_to_fix: 10 minutes
learnings:
  - Alphabetical sort does not handle semver — always use numeric comparison for version strings
  - Claude Code Skills v2 supports context:fork, agent routing, shell injection, allowed-tools, hooks — far beyond basic name+description
  - Shell injection (!`command`) is preprocessing, not Claude execution — output replaces the placeholder before the prompt is sent
  - Paradigm's existing agent types (architect, builder, reviewer, tester, security) map naturally to Skills v2 agent routing
  - 'The cross-study insight applies here: structured transparency (YAML frontmatter) over learned intelligence (neural routing) for skill dispatch'
